    "turn_text": "downtown buildings and houses. One of the things that, you know, we'll have to look at oversight and make sure things are going well. I'm on the Homeland Security Committee, which is FEMA is one of those agencies and we oversee there. And I've been pleased so far with the progress in terms of how they've been responding. So that's a positive sign. In terms of looking at the cost, there is some money already appropriated in the budget for disaster relief and supporting FEMA. There's still money left. The last I saw was about $3.3 billion out of the $7 billion appropriations. Now, the problem with that is you don't know what you don't know when you're budgeting for the year and figuring out what to appropriate. So obviously with a major catastrophe like this, it's going to be more than a typical year. And so we've got to come back and look at that. I haven't seen numbers that have been reported or that we're planning to look at, but obviously that'll be one of those things that we want to look at. One of the things that we have to guard against is the temptation when one of these must-passed bills like, you know, how do we do some disaster relief is you tend to get lots of pork barrel spending to get added to it as well. Superstorm sand, that was a perfect example of all of that wasteful spending that got added to it because people knew that had to pass. So that's one of those things you want to guard against. How do you go through that process and make sure that it's not",
