    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "over the office. That's right. Typically, what we do on the schedule is They'll build the whole schedule for the year in January. And most months, it's roughly three weeks in Washington, and we travel back and forth from the district. And then one week is in the district. And then, of course, August is that unique month where we're not scheduled to be doing anything in Washington. Most of us spend the time in the district. Of course, some members that have family before they go back to school do a family vacation or something. But it's been great opportunity to get out across the district this month. And we've gone to Farm Bureau dinners. We've gone to meetings with schools and businesses and, you know, holding town hall listening tours and stops around the district. I think what we're hearing is one, people want us to accomplish things in Washington. I mean, that's what they elected us for, particularly elected Republican House and Republican President and Republican majority in the Senate. And they want us to accomplish things. And so that's the big push that we need to keep working towards that. People in the district are still supportive of the president, and they want his agenda that they agree with. They voted for him for that reason. I mean, one of the problems he's struggling with right now is the Senate's way behind on confirming his nominees. And there's roughly 350 nominees that the president appoints that have to be confirmed by the Senate, and the cabinet secretaries and under secretaries and ambassadors. And so through June, only about 10% of those had been confirmed. And some of those haven't been made, though. Either they haven't received them",
