    "turn_idx": 23,
    "turn_text": "owners? I think several things that we're hearing. One is talking about trade and want to make sure that we maintain trade as much as we can because, you know, in the Fourth District, being able to export what we make here is a great value for us. It's a great value for Kansas, a great value for the country. And, of course, agriculture is part of that, but on the business side, in the business community, there's so many things we make here, whether it's airplanes or whether it's large airplanes made for Boeing and Airbus, or whether it's the general aviation airplanes that are made by Textron and Learjet. And even all the components and suppliers for not just the manufacturers here, but also across the country. And so want to make sure that we continue to support that. One of the things that's slowed down in the President's administration and actually I think starting to become a bit of a risk for us in the country is the length of time that took to confirm the trade representative kind of put us on pause, the United States on pause of how do we go through and work on trade deals across the world. And so I think some other countries were trying to jump in and get ahead of us in that regards. Now that they've got at least the representative in place, they don't have all of the supporting staff yet prepared, but they started off with NAFTA in terms of one of those arena. During the campaign, the president said some comments that really came across pretty negatively in terms of trade deals. I've been pleasantly surprised and pleased since the election that his terminologies changed not from tearing up contracts, but it's more towards how do we modernize contracts and how do we make sure that our trade agreements are fair? And that's the big thing that we ought to look at is free and fair trade and how do we handle that?",
