    "episode_id": 82797,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/2/82797.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 27,
    "turn_text": "and so looking at how do we make it viable for the 21st century and we need to make sure we keep those good components in place and go address those things that either weren't necessarily fair when they were originally included in that, or because conditions have changed, times change, and the world's changed. The internet wasn't a very valuable tool over 20 years ago, and now it is. So we need to upgrade those components and make sure we keep the good items as well as improving those areas that need to be improved. What are your biggest priorities when you get back into your DC office? When we get back in the house and we had a conference call last week with the House membership and with the speaker and leadership and talking about our priorities and where we're going to go. In September what we want to focus on is is getting a budget passed for fiscal year 18 and That's important But budgets not necessarily the way you and I might think of a budget at the federal level I mean the budgets really kind of a blueprint if you will but it but it sets up a Yardstick I guess in terms of spending that that's used going forward. So we're going to set that budget in place and That comes important down the road when we start talking about tax reform, and I'll talk about that in a minute, but so budget's first thing that we want to work on. Next thing is there are 12 appropriation bills that control the spending for the federal government. We've passed four of those already, and we're teed up to pass the other eight when we get back. If you look back the last 10 years or so, all 12 have not been passed in any year because you get a few passed and then to get caught up in the internal debate between the House and the Senate and the White House and things just didn't work. So they end up coming up with some big bloated omnibus spending bill and throw a lot of things into it to get enough votes. And so one of the things that we're really focusing on in the House this year is we want to make sure all 12 appropriations bills get passed so that we can then work with the Senate to get that moving forward. This is the first year of a presidential administration, so that whole process is a little bit slower because the president doesn't have his budget ready when he does the State of the Union. So it tends to be April, May. So that whole process gets pushed back a little bit. I don't expect that the House and Senate will have a final appropriation bill for all 12 appropriations done by the end of September. We're pushing for that, but if we don't, then we'll probably come up with a continuing resolution for a month or two. so that that can get finalized and get signed off by the president. So those are our first two priorities when we get back.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
