  {
    "episode_id": 82797,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/2/82797.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 31,
    "turn_text": "and the nuances there. That would be my expectation, which kind of says, okay, then let's wait until January and see what happens then because the things kind of get reset a little bit with a new calendar year. So we'll have new premium rates for people that'll kind of go up, be more expensive. People will have more pain in suffering through trying to figure out how do they pay for a premium. They do have insurance, how do you handle that, that large deductible so that you really sometimes can't get care because of the cost of that deductible and what you have to pay. The number of insurance companies that are pulling out of not offering services on an Obamacare exchange, just all of these things are failing. So we need to make sure that when we come, when 2018 rolls around that there'll be more pain and suffering. So that may be the time to take that up. One of the things we want to be concerned about is making sure that, you know, that we don't push the thought process that, well, let's just bail out the Obamacare exchanges and throw more money at it because that really doesn't solve the problem. And I'm sure that's going to be the message this fall if the Senate doesn't do anything. So we need to make sure we don't go down that route of doing additional bailouts for those Obamacare insurance exchanges. You're familiar with Josh",
